.TH YEW 1 "2026-10-12" "yew" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
yew, yew \- a modal terminal editor and its language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B yew
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ FILE ...]
.br
.B yew \-\-batch
.I SCRIPT
.RI [ FILE ...]
.RI [ \-\- " ARG" ...]
.br
.B yew fl
.RI [ FORM ]
.br
.B yew pkg
.I COMMAND
.br
.B yew plug
.I COMMAND
.br
.B yew syn
.I COMMAND
.br
.B yew tutor
.RI [ CHAPTER ]
.RI [ \-\-list ]
.RI [ \-\-reset ]
.RI [ \-\-check ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
yew is a modal, keyboard-first terminal text editor.
Its single-threaded event loop multiplexes terminal input, jobs, language
servers, and timers without blocking the editing core.
The implementation is C11 and depends on the C and POSIX libraries rather
than an application framework.
.PP
Fletch is yew's configuration and automation language.
Bindings, hooks, batch scripts, plugins, and recorded macros meet at the same
named-command dispatch surface, and a recording is readable Fletch source
rather than an opaque keystroke file.
.PP
yew is not a vim clone and not an IDE.
It has its own arrow-key modal model, composes external tools through jobs,
and accepts extension code only as Fletch; native or binary plugins are not
loaded.
.SH MODES
The status line displays the one-letter chip shown below.
.TP
.B L \(em line
The line unit.
Left and Right move to line bounds; Up and Down move between logical lines.
Escape stays in or returns to L.
.TP
.B W \(em word
The Unicode word unit.
Left and Right move between words; vertical arrows retain the display goal.
Escape returns to L.
.TP
.B B \(em block
The syntax-aware block unit, with deterministic structural fallbacks when no
syntax block is available.
Arrows move among block boundaries and matching bounds.
Escape returns to L.
.TP
.B H \(em highlight
The selection action mode.
It retains the unit that entered it: line, word, block, or insert-character.
Escape clears selections and extra cursors, then returns to L.
.TP
.B I \(em insert
Text keys insert UTF-8 text and arrows move by grapheme or display line.
Escape first dismisses a live shadow suggestion; otherwise it returns to L.
.TP
.B E \(em execute
The command, search, shell, and answer prompt.
Escape cancels the prompt and restores the mode that opened it.
The command registry supplies completion, argument policy, and dispatch.
.TP
.B F \(em fuss
The workspace and Git drawer.
Arrows navigate the visible tree, ordinary filename characters perform
type-to-jump over visible rows, and Alt-modified action keys operate on the
selected path.
Escape closes the drawer and returns to L without changing the active file.
.SH KEYS
Key names use
.B C-
for Control,
.B A-
for Alt,
.B S-
for Shift, and angle brackets for named terminal keys.
A space inside a key name separates a chord.
Counts are accepted by L, W, and B where the invoked command permits one.
The following inventory is literal checked-in documentation: each
.B BIND
row is
.IR "mode | key | registered command | optional bound argument" .
The special mode
.B *
installs the row in L, W, and B.
.PP
The compiled emergency layer remains beneath this runtime map.
If configuration cannot load, L still provides Escape,
.BR q ,
.BR "q !" ,
.BR : ,
and
.BR ! ;
E still provides Enter and Escape; and F retains navigation and its
Alt-modified action table.
