.PP
Command names may also be entered by their unique short-name prefix or full
registered name.
Special shell spellings are
.B :!command
to run a job,
.B :range!command
to filter a range,
.B :r!command
to read output, and
.B :!!command
to hand the real terminal to a foreground child.
Shell output opens a named read-only temporary tab, never replacing a
document pane. Escape, :q, and ordinary tab-close dismiss the view and return
to its originating tab. The job continues after its view closes; :jobs can
reopen the output.
.SS "Git input and output"
FUSS input-taking actions use centered modals. Enter accepts a single-line
prompt; in commit messages and rebase todo/message edits it inserts a newline.
Ctrl-S accepts either form. Escape cancels without accepting the typed text.
Repeating a dialog's activation shortcut also cancels it (for example,
Alt-m opens the commit modal and Alt-m again discards it). This also applies
to list/group pickers, context menus and confirmations. Remapped chords are
honored; holding the opening key does not toggle the dialog.
Failed commits retain their drafts and open a separate error-output tab.
Interactive rebase uses Git's todo and message files through an asynchronous
editor bridge instead of starting a nested terminal editor. Cancelling a
rebase editor step does not silently discard a partial rebase; use
git.rebase.continue or git.rebase.abort after inspecting its state.
Git output tabs close like shell-output tabs and restore FUSS on return.
.SH "REGULAR EXPRESSIONS"
yew's engine is a Thompson NFA/DFA hybrid with leftmost-first semantics and
a linear-time design.
Patterns and inputs operate on Unicode codepoints, not bytes or grapheme
clusters.
Invalid UTF-8 input bytes remain lossless surrogate-escape codepoints; they
match dot and negated classes, but not word, digit, or space classes.
.PP
The accepted syntax is:
.TP
.B literals and escapes
Non-metacharacters match themselves.
Backslash quotes metacharacters and supports
.BR \en ,
.BR \et ,
.BR \er ,
.BR \ef ,
.BR \ev ,
.BR \ea ,
.BR \ee ,
.BR \e0 ,
.BR \exHH ,
and
.BR \ex{H...} .
.TP
.B .
Match any codepoint except newline; dotall or
.B (?s)
also matches newline.
.TP
.B [abc] [a-z] [^...]
Character classes and negated classes.
Inside a class,
.B ]
is literal first and
.B -
is literal first or last.
Class escapes and POSIX bracket classes may be combined.
.TP
.B \ew \eW \ed \eD \es \eS
Unicode word, non-word, decimal-digit, non-digit, white-space, and
non-space classes.
.TP
.B [[:name:]] [[:^name:]]
POSIX classes
.BR alpha ,
.BR digit ,
.BR alnum ,
.BR upper ,
.BR lower ,
.BR space ,
.BR blank ,
.BR punct ,
.BR cntrl ,
.BR graph ,
.BR print ,
.BR xdigit ,
and
.BR word .
.TP
.B \eb \eB
A boundary, or non-boundary, between
.B \ew
and non-word codepoints, including buffer edges.
.TP
.B ^ $
Line anchors unconditionally; there is no multiline flag.
.B $
matches before newline, at the search-window end, and before the carriage
return of a CRLF pair.
.TP
.B \eA \ez
Beginning and end of the complete search window.
.TP
.B (...) (?:...)
Capturing and non-capturing groups.
There are at most 32 groups including group zero.
.TP
.B (?i) (?-i) (?s) (?-s) (?i:...) (?s:...)
Inline and group-scoped case-insensitive and dotall flags.
.TP
.B a|b
Alternation.
Earlier branches win ties, so
.B a|ab
against
.B ab
matches
.BR a .
.TP
.B * + ? {m} {m,} {m,n}
Greedy quantifiers.
Appending
.B ?
makes a quantifier lazy.
Repeat bounds are at most 1000.
.PP
The four Unicode definitions used together are:
.B \ew
= Alphabetic union Nd union Pc;
.B \ed
= general category Nd;
.B \es
= Unicode White_Space; and
.B \eb
= a transition defined from
.BR \ew .
Case-insensitive matching uses Unicode simple folding only.
Length-changing full folds such as sharp-s to
.B ss
are deliberately excluded because one VM step consumes one codepoint.
There is no locale-dependent matching and no normalization.
.PP
Lookaround, pattern backreferences, atomic and possessive groups, recursion,
conditionals,
.BR \eG ,
and
.B \eK
are rejected.
These features require backtracking or nested simulations and would forfeit
the guarantee that a pattern typed into incremental search cannot hang the
editor.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Normal startup loads the shipped
.IR runtime/init.fl ,
then the user
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/yew/init.fl ,
then
.I <workspace>/.yew.fl
after its trust decision.
.B \-\-config
replaces the user file;
.B \-\-no-workspace-config
skips the workspace file; and
.B \-\-clean
skips all file-based configuration.
.PP
Fletch functions
.BR set() ,
.BR bind() ,
and
.B on()
configure options, key sequences, and hooks.
The option registry is the authority for type, scope, default, completion,
and module ownership; E-mode
.B :set
and Fletch use that same table.
A workspace file is code and is never trusted because of its pathname alone.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/yew/init.fl
User configuration.
The fallback is
.IR $HOME/.config/yew/init.fl .
.TP
